         ]
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Take \\( f(x) = \\frac{\\ln x}{x^{2}} \\), so \\( f(n) \\) is the general term. On \\( [2, \\infty) \\), \\( f \\) is positive, continuous, and decreasing, so the integral test applies.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Ln",
               "x"
            ],
            [
               "Power",
               "x",
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The series starts at \\( n = 2 \\), so the matching integral is \\( \\int_{2}^{\\infty} \\frac{\\ln x}{x^{2}} \\, dx = \\lim_{b \\to \\infty} \\int_{2}^{b} \\frac{\\ln x}{x^{2}} \\, dx \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "An antiderivative is \\( F(x) = \\frac{- \\ln{\\left(x \\right)} - 1}{x} \\), so \\( \\int_{2}^{b} \\frac{\\ln x}{x^{2}} \\, dx = \\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{- \\ln{\\left(b \\right)} - 1}{b} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Divide",
               [
                  "Add",
                  [
                     "Negate",
                     [
                        "Ln",
                        "b"
                     ]
                  ],
                  -1
               ],
               "b"
            ],
            [
               "Rational",
               1,
               2
            ],
            [
               "Divide",
               [
                  "Ln",
                  2
               ],
               2
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "As \\( b \\to \\infty \\), \\( F(b) \\to 0 \\), so the integral converges to \\( \\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2} \\). This number is the value of the integral, not the sum of the series.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
